This guide will walk you through setting up a NixOS system using Flakes and Home Manager, starting from a fresh NixOS installation. We'll configure GNOME with Wayland, install Neovim, VS Code, Zed, Brave Browser, Bitwarden, and several common command-line tools.

**Assumptions:**

*   You have successfully installed NixOS.
*   You have internet connectivity.
*   You are comfortable using the command line and editing text files.
*   You have root access (using `sudo`).

**Step 1: Initial Setup and Directory Preparation**

After installation, NixOS generates initial configuration files in `/etc/nixos/`. We will modify these and add our Flake and Home Manager files there.

1.  **Navigate to the configuration directory:**
    ```bash
    cd /etc/nixos
    ```
2.  **Backup existing configuration (optional but recommended):**
    ```bash
    sudo cp configuration.nix configuration.nix.backup
    sudo cp hardware-configuration.nix hardware-configuration.nix.backup
    ```

**Step 2: Create `flake.nix`**

This file defines the inputs (like Nixpkgs and Home Manager) and outputs (your system configuration) for your Flake.

Create a new file named `flake.nix` in `/etc/nixos/` with the following content. Remember to replace `"your-hostname"` with the actual hostname you want for your machine (it should match the `networking.hostName` setting in `configuration.nix`). Also, replace `"your-username"` with your actual username.

```nix
# /etc/nixos/flake.nix
{
  description = "My NixOS Flake Configuration";

  inputs = {
    # Nix Packages collection
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";

    # Home Manager
    home-manager = {
      url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
      # Make Home Manager use the same Nixpkgs as the system
      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
    };
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, home-manager, ... }@inputs: {
    nixosConfigurations = {
      # Replace "your-hostname" with your actual system hostname
      "your-hostname" = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
        system = "x86_64-linux"; # Adjust if you use a different architecture
        specialArgs = { inherit inputs; }; # Pass inputs to modules
        modules = [
          # Import the main system configuration
          ./configuration.nix

          # Integrate Home Manager module
          home-manager.nixosModules.home-manager
          {
            home-manager.useGlobalPkgs = true; # Use system Nixpkgs
            home-manager.useUserPackages = true; # Allow user-specific packages

            # Define the user and import their Home Manager config
            # Replace "your-username" with your actual username
            home-manager.users."your-username" = import ./home.nix;

            # Optionally pass inputs to home.nix
            home-manager.extraSpecialArgs = { inherit inputs; };
          }
        ];
      };
    };
  };
}
```

**Step 3: Create `home.nix`**

This file defines the user-specific configuration managed by Home Manager. Create `home.nix` in `/etc/nixos/`. Replace `"your-username"` and `"/home/your-username"` accordingly.

```nix
# /etc/nixos/home.nix
{ pkgs, config, lib, ... }:

{
  # Set home-manager state version
  home.stateVersion = "23.11"; # Or the version you are currently using

  # Define user and home directory
  home.username = "your-username";
  home.homeDirectory = "/home/your-username";

  # Let Home Manager manage itself
  programs.home-manager.enable = true;

  # Packages to install for the user
  home.packages = with pkgs; [
    # Editors
    neovim
    vscode # Provided by nixpkgs-unfree, enable allowUnfree = true in configuration.nix
    zed-editor # Provided by nixpkgs-unfree

    # Browser & Password Manager
    brave      # Provided by nixpkgs-unfree
    bitwarden-desktop # Or bitwarden for the CLI

    # Add other user-specific tools if needed
    # Example: ripgrep fd
  ];

  # Enable and configure Neovim
  programs.neovim = {
    enable = true;
    viAlias = true; # Alias vi to nvim
    vimAlias = true; # Alias vim to nvim
    # Basic configuration example (add more as needed)
    extraConfig = ''
      set number relativenumber
      set tabstop=2 shiftwidth=2 expandtab
      set ignorecase smartcase
    '';
    # Example plugins (requires adding vimPlugins to the input 'pkgs' above if needed separately)
    # plugins = with pkgs.vimPlugins; [ fugitive ];
  };

  # Example: Basic Git configuration
  programs.git = {
    enable = true;
    userName = "Your Name";
    userEmail = "your.email@example.com";
  };

  # Example: Enable VS Code (optional, can also be configured via settings.json)
  programs.vscode = {
    enable = true;
    # You can manage extensions here if desired
    # extensions = with pkgs.vscode-extensions; [ editorconfig.editorconfig ];
    # userSettings = { "editor.fontSize" = 14; };
  };

  # Allow unfree packages within Home Manager as well
  # This might be needed depending on how inputs are passed and inherited
  nixpkgs.config.allowUnfree = true;

  # Optional: Configure GTK theme (if needed beyond GNOME defaults)
  # gtk = {
  #   enable = true;
  #   theme.name = "Adwaita-dark";
  #   iconTheme.name = "Adwaita";
  # };
}

```

**Step 4: Modify `configuration.nix`**

Now, edit your main system configuration file, `/etc/nixos/configuration.nix`. Integrate the settings for Flakes, GNOME, Wayland, system packages, and your user account.

```nix
