    std::cout << "\nExiting main..." << std::endl;
    // myList goes out of scope here, nodes 20 and 30 are automatically destroyed.
    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation:**
This code uses `std::unique_ptr` within the `Node` struct to manage the pointer to the `next` node. Each `Node` exclusively owns the subsequent node in the list. The `LinkedList` class itself holds a `std::unique_ptr` to the `head` node. When a `LinkedList` object is destroyed (e.g., goes out of scope), its `head` unique_ptr is destroyed. This automatically calls `delete` on the head `Node`. The destructor of the head `Node` then destroys its `next` unique_ptr, which in turn deletes the second node, and this chain reaction continues until the entire list is deallocated. Operations like `pop_front` also leverage `std::move` to transfer ownership, ensuring the removed node is automatically deleted. This mechanism provides automatic memory management based on object lifetime and ownership (RAII), not traditional garbage collection.

